  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Thursday...\n\nShowers and embedded thunderstorms will move in from the \nsouthwest this afternoon and evening, with an area of showers \ndeveloping over South Central Montana overnight and continuing\ninto the weekend. Heavy rain is anticipated, particularly tonight\nthrough Saturday, and a Flood Watch is in effect this evening \nthrough Sunday afternoon for South Central Montana, much of \nSoutheast Montana, and portions of Wyoming, including Sheridan \nCounty. Over northern Wyoming into Southeast Montana, occasional \nrounds of precipitation are expected before an area of stronger \nshowers and thunderstorms move through Saturday afternoon. Recent\nburn scars will be highly susceptible to flash flooding and \ndebris flows. Those living near recent burn scars should have a \nplan of action to seek safety should heavy rainfall develop in \nthose locations. Stay aware of the directions of local officials \nand any Flash Flood Warnings that may be issued. \n\nCurrently, there is a widespread 50-95% chance (highest \nwhere there is a Flood Watch) of at least 1 inch of precipitation \nand a 50-80% chance of at least 2 inches from east of Livingston \nthrough Miles City through Sunday evening. Locally higher amounts\nof 4+ inches will be possible for areas where slow moving \nthunderstorms develop or where thunderstorms repeatedly move \nthrough.\n\nThe risk for severe thunderstorms today is low, although a \nstronger storm or two may develop late this evening over Southeast\nMontana. The main threat remains heavy rain, and wind gusts up to\n50 mph will be possible with any stronger storm.\n\nSnow levels fall to around 11 kft by Saturday afternoon, allowing\na few inches of snow over the highest peaks. During the overnight\nhours tonight and Saturday night, a dusting of snow (30% chance) \nmay fall on the Beartooth Pass.\n\nAfter the system exits the region Sunday, conditions trend warmer\nand drier. Highs over the weekend will generally be in the 60s,\nincreasing to low 70s by Monday and near 80 by midweek. Archer",
